50 730 Td (C4 AND CAM PLANTS) Tj
50 712 Td () Tj
50 694 Td (C4 plants such as corn and sugarcane have a specialized anatomy called Kranz anatomy. They fix) Tj
50 676 Td (carbon dioxide in mesophyll cells into a four-carbon compound, oxaloacetate, using an enzyme) Tj
50 658 Td (called PEP carboxylase. This compound is transported to bundle sheath cells, where carbon) Tj
50 640 Td (dioxide is released and enters the Calvin cycle.) Tj
50 622 Td () Tj
50 604 Td (Because PEP carboxylase does not react with oxygen, C4 plants can concentrate carbon dioxide) Tj
50 586 Td (around rubisco and avoid photorespiration, making them efficient in hot, dry climates.) Tj
50 568 Td () Tj
50 550 Td (CAM plants, such as cacti and succulents, collect carbon dioxide at night when stomata are) Tj
50 532 Td (open, converting it to an organic acid stored in vacuoles. During the day, stomata close to) Tj
50 514 Td (conserve water, and the stored acid releases carbon dioxide for the Calvin cycle. This) Tj
50 496 Td (temporal separation lets CAM plants survive extreme desert conditions.) Tj
ET
endstream
